{
  "threads": [
    {
      "index": 1,
      "rootCommentId": 4711253719,
      "threadId": "PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc68fAii",
      "path": "scripts/release_lazy_draft.sh",
      "line": 163,
      "resolved": false,
      "bot": false,
      "body": "⚠️ severity: IMPORTANT —\n⚠️ **IMPORTANT: the 5xx retry can mint a same-marker twin draft that `release_draft_twin_dedupe.sh` cannot resolve — reproducing the exact sweep recurrence this PR closes**\n\nThe retry heals a landed-500 only when the draft becomes visible **at the retry boundary** — but the PR's own root cause says the v1.0.778 draft \"resolved **minutes later**\". In that exact shape the sequence is:\n\n1. Attempt 1 gets `HTTP 500` but **lands server-side** (draft D1 created, not yet visible).\n2. The failure-boundary re-view (`gh release view`) still sees nothing, the classifier matches `HTTP 5xx`, and the loop retries here (`scripts/release_lazy_draft.sh:163`).\n3. GitHub does not enforce one-release-per-tag for **drafts** (gh-2372/gh-1899), so attempt 2's create **succeeds** and mints D2. Both D1 and D2 carry the **same** `release-draft-owner: run/<id>/job/<name>` marker (same leg, same `RELEASE_DRAFT_IDENTITY`).\n4. The success path runs `release_draft_twin_dedupe.sh` — but its winner election (`scripts/release_draft_twin_dedupe.sh`, the `winner` jq predicate) only elects an **older** same-tag release (`.id < mine_min`, published or pipeline-created). Between two same-marker twins there is no such object: `mine_min` is our own older draft and nothing precedes it → \"our draft for $tag is the oldest same-tag release — it stays\" and **both drafts survive**.\n\nConsequence: the tag now resolves ambiguously for the attach gates and for release-provenance (assets land in one draft, the publish can flip the other — the gh-2372 twin-rot class); the surviving twin rots 24h+ until the daily sweeper deletes it and **re-files \"[daily-publish] stale release drafts swept\"** — the exact recurrence gh-2432 is about, now reachable through the fix itself. The pinned landed-500 test (`viewFoundAfterFirst: true`) only covers the fast-resolution shape where the re-view sees the draft immediately.\n\n**Suggested fix**: extend `release_draft_twin_dedupe.sh` — when ≥2 own-marker drafts exist, elect the **oldest own draft** as the winner and delete our own **newer** twins by id (both carry this job's exact marker, so this stays inside the existing \"a job only ever deletes drafts it just created\" invariant; mirrors the existing external-winner delete loop). Then pin it with a fixture case where the 500's view stays not-found past the boundary (`GH_STUB_CREATE_FAILS_5XX_N: '1'` **without** `viewFoundAfterFirst`), asserting exactly one same-marker draft survives the create+dedupe.\n\nEvidence that would raise this to 🚨: a fixture reproduction showing two same-marker drafts surviving a 500-then-success create (I could not run that against the real API from review, but every step above is grounded in the PR's own root-cause narrative and the dedupe's election code)."
    }
  ],
  "census": {
